202d0cf913e4397546006c7c38b2b70bb634b860aa35720" :answer-field/choices ["q-353435/field-1/9ff8157b20b64a245202d0cf913e4397546006c7c38b2b70bb634b860aa35720" "q-353435/field-1/695792c47319cb1ed098a6d83038b7ad6d032838f9103d693bf346f1dc234ff8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-353435" :question/id #uuid "b81a0b77-b6af-5353-a3e2-62c0a28081d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-353435" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(t),$ what is the carrying capacity of the following logistic growth model?\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3600y-9{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=40\n$$\n\nThe carrying capacity is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/d8f19dcb707da9aac0ff59b8127778b8b45ef4f55f3fa404b3e589e323c03905.png)\n\nFirst, let's factor the right-hand side of the given equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=9y(400-y),\\,y(0)=40.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=400.$ Therefore, the carrying capacity is $400.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-353435/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "91856ca3-4d9c-51c4-910b-27acba52156e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-353435"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-118577/selection/c5b7aea79f73ebc562cbcff24725697bdc076b45efa2d86abecbb1b4fa682579" :answer/id #uuid "dc67f1dd-3f0b-5228-a25f-84f264f0f694" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "600" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118577/selection/9ff8157b20b64a245202d0cf913e4397546006c7c38b2b70bb634b860aa35720" :answer/id #uuid "e4aad8df-7c42-5760-a2c5-a342c292f9be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "400" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118577/selection/62a